      "abstract": "Comprehensive results on the production of unidentified charged particles, ${\\ensuremath{\\pi}}^{\\ifmmode\\pm\\else\\textpm\\fi{}}, {\\mathrm{K}}^{\\ifmmode\\pm\\else\\textpm\\fi{}}, {\\mathrm{K}}_{\\mathrm{S}}^{0}, {\\mathrm{K}}^{*}{(892)}^{0}, p, \\overline{p}, \\ensuremath{\\phi}$(1020), $\\mathrm{\\ensuremath{\\Lambda}}, \\overline{\\mathrm{\\ensuremath{\\Lambda}}}, {\\mathrm{\\ensuremath{\\Xi}}}^{\\ensuremath{-}}, {\\overline{\\mathrm{\\ensuremath{\\Xi}}}}^{+}, {\\mathrm{\\ensuremath{\\Omega}}}^{\\ensuremath{-}}$, and ${\\overline{\\mathrm{\\ensuremath{\\Omega}}}}^{+}$ hadrons in proton-proton ($pp$) collisions at $\\sqrt{s}=7$ TeV at midrapidity ($|y|<0.5$) as a function of charged-particle multiplicity density are presented. In order to avoid autocorrelation biases, the actual transverse momentum (${p}_{\\mathrm{T}}$) spectra of the particles under study and the event activity are measured in different rapidity windows. In the highest multiplicity class, the charged-particle density reaches about 3.5 times the value measured in inelastic collisions. While the yield of protons normalized to pions remains approximately constant as a function of multiplicity, the corresponding ratios of strange hadrons to pions show...",
